{"kind":"report","text":"Ready for review at exact clean head 203a8ab8875c440c41474d103c3715a1aab5e4be on task/ui-interpretation-in-context.\n\nConditions met. An act the room cannot read explains itself on that act: the fold's reason, verbatim, and what the refusal cost - for an undefined kind, that any promise, report or citation it was written to establish never formed; for an uninterpretable act, that it is without force until the interpreter is bound. The aggregate panel that counted them is deleted. The unbound fold is stated in the Vocabulary panel, where the room's interpretive scope already lives, in the register used for other stated limits rather than as an incident.\n\nThe judgement the request asked for - can a reader tell, without leaving the surface, whether anything is owed - is answered on the act and, for the two acts that prompted this, the answer is no: their commitments are withdrawn and their heads are ancestors of main, and nothing in the surface now implies otherwise.\n\nI did not solve this by hiding interpretation gaps. A live gap is still shown on the act it concerns, which is where someone waiting on that act will be looking; what was removed is the count divorced from consequence.\n\nOne thing found while implementing, beyond the request as written. workSummary was adding interpretation gaps and the unbound fold into the header chip's stale count - the danger-tinted badge meaning work needs attention. Removing only the panel would have left a standing design limit and two inert acts still inflating that badge, which is the same error one layer up. The stale count is now workAttentionCount alone.\n\nTwo changes I want a reviewer to judge rather than accept.\n\nFirst, Stream read document.title during render, so it threw under server rendering and could not be exercised by the existing harness at all. This is the cause another reviewer named on 019f08ba when reporting that everything left inside Stream is pinned by nothing. The request requires a rendered test of an act, and acts render in Stream, so this was a precondition rather than scope creep. I guarded the read - typeof document === undefined - rather than restructuring the title behaviour, which is the smallest change that makes the component renderable. The effects that write document.title are untouched and still browser-only.\n\nSecond, I replaced an existing test rather than adding beside it. The old test pinned how gaps were grouped into panel rows, one row per distinct refusal; that behaviour no longer exists, so the test could only be deleted or rewritten. Its replacement pins the new contract: an unreadable act yields a reason and a consequence, and an ordinary ineffective verdict yields no notice at all.\n\nVerified. tsc clean; npm test 43 of 43; npm run build with the committed bundle regenerated in this commit; make vet and make test pass. Three mutations each turn the new rendered test red, checked one at a time with restoration between: deleting the on-act explanation, demoting the reason and consequence back to a hover title, and counting unreadable acts as attention again. The test therefore fails for each half of what it claims separately, not only for their conjunction.\n\nNot done. I did not touch the write path - warning an author who uses a kind the room cannot read is request f13441a5 and belongs to it. No browser click-through; the evidence is server-rendered markup, as it is for every UI review in this repository.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6426bf362eca5a6f091cb1b7110023fac9c4c650","branch":"task/ui-interpretation-in-context","head":"203a8ab8875c440c41474d103c3715a1aab5e4be"}}